{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Six years, etc.</b> It intends to establish a positive commandment for sowing, etc., and for pruning in the seventh year, for a prohibition that issues from the general category of a positive commandment is itself a positive commandment (Pesahim 41a). And although the Rambam wrote in the Laws of Shemittah (chapter 1) that the positive commandment is derived from the statement ״וְשָׁבְתָה הָאָרֶץ שַׁבָּת״ etc., even so, from there one does not hear a positive commandment for sowing and a positive commandment for pruning. Or perhaps it comes to obligate him in two positive commandments. And see the verse ״וְאָסַפְתָּ״." "<b>And for six years you shall prune.</b> It repeated saying ״וְשֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים״ and did not suffice with saying ״שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים תִּזְרַע שָׂדֶךָ וְתִזְמֹר כַּרְמֶךָ״. This is explained according to the statement of the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, in chapter 5 of Shevi'it, and this is their language: saplings of the seventh year that are in their second year, etc. And they gave the reason because with trees we follow the stage of budding, and since they budded in the seventh year their law is like the fruits of the seventh year and they are forbidden in the second year to the seventh year. See that with trees we follow budding, while with grain and vegetables we follow harvesting, as they said in the first chapter of Rosh HaShanah (13a). Therefore HaShem distinguished and said: six years of field seeds in their own right, and six years of the vineyard in its own right, because their law is not the same — for this we follow harvesting and for that we follow budding." "<b>And you shall gather, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״וְאָסַפְתָּ״. Perhaps it intended to make a fine point of <b>וְאָסַפְתָּ אֶת תְּבוּאָתָהּ</b> and not its aftergrowth that grows in the seventh year, and the intention in this is to establish a positive commandment for gathering aftergrowth, for a prohibition that issues from the general category of a positive commandment is itself a positive commandment. Or it may intend to promise that even though the land was sown for six years, and according to the nature of the land when sown year after year it does not yield its strength, as they said, whose memory is for blessing (Bava Batra 29a): one who sows one year and leaves it fallow one year, etc. — in this manner it yields its strength, but year upon year, and especially six years, the land will surely fail; and a person might say: behold, it is very good that the land rest in one pattern, in an order that allows it to yield its strength in the year of sowing, and this matter has no boundary to make the seventh year precisely a sabbatical year. Therefore He said <b>שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים</b>, and even so <b>וְאָסַפְתָּ אֶת תְּבוּאָתָהּ</b> in the manner proper for it without weakening from year to year, and there is no need to leave it fallow during any of the six years, for HaShem will command the blessing in the Holy Land. Or perhaps this too was intended by His saying <b>דַּבֵּר וְאָמַרְתָּ</b> — דַּבֵּר according to its plain sense, and אָמַרְתָּ: for on the contrary, a good gift I am giving them, that they may sow the field, etc., six years one after another, and the land will not fail to give its strength equally, without their needing to leave it fallow other than the seventh year by decree of the King." "<b>Further,</b> it is explained according to what they said in Sanhedrin page 26, and this is their language: Rabbi Yannai announces: go out and sow in the seventh year because of the land tax. And the Tosafot wrote: and if you say, because of the land tax they permitted plowing in the seventh year by Torah law? And one may answer, etc. Furthermore one may say that it is a matter of pikuach nefesh, for the ruler demands it from him and he does not have it, etc. — thus far. And this is the meaning of the verse: <b>שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים</b> etc. <b>וְאָסַפְתָּ</b> etc. — meaning: when do I not permit you except six and not seven? ״וְאָסַפְתָּ״ — when you sow in order to gather for yourself; but for the land tax, to give to the ruler — sow even in the seventh year, according to the words of Rabbi Yannai."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:08.040484377Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}